    "evidence_text": "We present the first system for this problem applied to a realistic multi-speaker dataset, the CALLHOME Spanish-English speech translation corpus.\n\nIntroduction\tTypical speech-to-text translation systems pipeline automatic speech recognition (ASR) and machine translation (MT)  .\n\nNevertheless, there are important low-resource settings in which even limited speech translation would\n\nIn these settings, target translations may be available.\n\nCan we do anything at all with this data?\n\nOur work complements several lines of related recent work.\n\nworking with audio, they started from word or phoneme lattices.\n\nAdditionally, their evaluation focused on phone error rate rather than translation.\n\nin cross-speaker clustering (\u00a7 SECREF4 ).\n\nUsing additional (not only training) audio in UTD increases the likelihood of discovering more clusters.\n\nThis is fair since the UTD has access to only the audio.\n\nWe ran UTD over all 104 telephone\n\ncalls, which pair 11 hours of audio with Spanish transcripts and their crowdsourced English translations.\n\nAlthough the utterance-level split is optimistic, it allows us to investigate how multiple speakers affect system performance.\n\nIn either\n\ncase, the oracle has about 38k Spanish tokens to train on.\n\nSuch inconsistencies in turn affect the translation distribution conditioned on c2.\n\nMost matches in our corpus are across calls, yet these are also the least accurate (Table TABREF9 ).\n\nWithin-call matches fall in between.\n\nOverall, average\n\nUTD is\n\nWe found that the patterns discovered by ZRTools match only 28% of the audio.\n\nFor almost half the utterances, UTD fails to produce any pseudoterm at all.",
